WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.842
State is the working record of the workflow.

00:00:02.842 --> 00:00:08.247
Suppose the user asks, can I return order one zero four two?

00:00:08.247 --> 00:00:11.182
The state might keep the original question.

00:00:11.182 --> 00:00:17.182
It may also keep a route indicating that we need both policy evidence and order data.

00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:22.207
After retrieval, the state contains the relevant policy chunks.

00:00:22.207 --> 00:00:26.417
After the order tool runs, it contains the delivery date.

00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:30.041
The step counter tells us how many actions have happened.

00:00:30.712 --> 00:00:35.134
Why save these values? Because later steps need them.

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:39.349
The final generation step needs the policy and order data.

00:00:39.349 --> 00:00:42.144
The stopping logic needs the step count.

00:00:42.144 --> 00:00:45.954
Citation validation needs source identifiers.

00:00:45.954 --> 00:00:53.164
A common project mistake is storing everything, including huge prompts and temporary strings.

00:00:53.164 --> 00:00:57.640
That makes state harder to debug and may make persistence expensive.

00:00:58.236 --> 00:01:05.498
A simple rule is: if another step genuinely needs the value later, state is a reasonable place for it.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:10.251
If the value is only temporary inside one function, keep it local.
